targetStatementsAndProofs.statement:
Define $E(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} z^n/n!$ for complex $z$; the series converges absolutely for every $z$, and $E(z+w)=E(z)E(w)$. For real $x$ write $e^x=E(x)$ (this agrees with $e=E(1)$ and with rational powers of $e$). Then:
(a) $e^x$ is continuous and differentiable on $\R$;
(b) $(e^x)'=e^x$;
(c) $e^x$ is strictly increasing and positive;
(d) $e^{x+y}=e^xe^y$;
(e) $e^x\to+\infty$ as $x\to+\infty$ and $e^x\to0$ as $x\to-\infty$;
(f) $\lim_{x\to+\infty}x^ne^{-x}=0$ for every $n$.

Consequently $e^x$ has an inverse $\log:(0,\infty)\to\R$, with $(\log y)'=1/y$ and $\log(uv)=\log u+\log v$; it is used to define $x^\alpha=e^{\alpha\log x}$ for $x>0$.

Suppose $X,Y\ge0$, or $E|X|<\infty$ and $E|Y|<\infty$. Then

(a) $E(X+Y)=EX+EY$;
(b) $E(aX+b)=a\,EX+b$ for all real $a,b$;
(c) if $X\ge Y$, then $EX\ge EY$.
